    "explanation": "(1) Nectin 4 is expressed in many cancer cells, such as urothelial carcinoma cells　=>正確\n\nNectin-4（Nectin Cell Adhesion Molecule 4）\n\nNectin-4 (Nectin cell adhesion molecule 4) is a cell adhesion molecule of the Nectin and Nectin-like families that is highly abundant in normal embryonic and fetal tissues and poorly expressed in healthy adult tissues. (Normal tissues including skin, salivary glands, bladder and esophagus express only low to medium levels of Nectin-4)\n\nSolid tumors that highly express Nectin-4 include bladder cancer, pancreatic cancer, triple-negative breast cancer, non-small cell lung cancer, gastric cancer, esophageal cancer, ovarian cancer, and many other cancers.\n\n(2) Nectin 4 is a membranous protein　=>正確\n\nNectin-4 is a type I membrane glycoprotein that consists of an extracellular region with three Ig domains of Ig V and IgC types. \n(See Figure.1)\n\n(3) Membranous Nectin-4 expression frequently decreases during metastatic spread of urothelial carcinoma => 正確\n\n(April 2024, ASCO)\nKlümper, N. et. al. assessed Membranous NECTIN-4 protein expression (H-score by Immunohistochemistry (IHC)) between primary tumors (PRIM) and patient-matched distant metastases (MET), they noted that membranous NECTIN-4 expression significantly decreased during metastatic spread, with 39.4% of MET lacking membranous NECTIN-4 expression. (The other EV-treated cohort of 47 patients also suggested this decreasing is associated with Enfortumab-Vedotin resistance in metastatic UC) \n\n(4)  Cancer cells with small cell carcinoma differentiation express less.  => 正確\n\nN=169 bladder cancer patients received nectin-4 IHC testing, 15 of them had small cell carcinoma histology, all nectin-4 IHC were megative. using whole-transcriptome RNA sequencing found that small cell carcinomas and sarcomatoid carcinomas expressed low levels of Nectin-4 mRNA.\n\n*Other histologies and Nectin-4 positive(H-score > 15) rate in this study: \nNon-muscle invasive urothelial carcinoma(N=72) : 87% ; \nSquamous(N=10): 70%; \nPlasmacytoid (N=8): 62.5% \nMicropapillary(N=11): 28%\nGlandulr (N=6) : 66%\nNested(N=4) : 50% \nSarcomatoid (N=10) : 10% \n\n<Ref>\n1. Klümper, N.et. al. (2023)  Clinical cancer research : an official journal of the American Association for Cancer Research, 29(8), 1496–1505. https://doi.org/10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-22-1764\n2. Nectin-4-Directed Drugs for Solid Tumors | Biopharma PEG. (n.d.). https://www.biochempeg.com/article/288.html\n3. Hoffman-Censits et. al.(2021) Appl Immunohistochem Mol Morphol. 1;29(8):619-625.",
    "questionTextFromExplanationFile": "83. Nectin 4 is an important target for drug design for urothelial carcinoma. Which of the following\nstatements are (is) correct ?\n(1) Nectin 4 is expressed in many cancer cells, such as urothelial carcinoma cells.\n(2) Nectin 4 is a membranous protein\n(3) Membranous Nectin-4 expression frequently decreases during metastatic spread of urothelial\ncarcinoma\n(4) Cancer cells with small cell carcinoma differentiation express less.\n(A) (1),(2),(3)\n(B) (1),(3)\n(C) (2),(4)\n(D) (4)\n(E) (1),(2),(3),(4)",
